## 1. Executive Summary

This plan sets out how I will analyse the **Information Technology (IT) sector** of the US equity market, using the S&P 500 as the benchmark. IT was chosen because it is the largest and most influential sector by market value, it is highly sensitive to interest rates and earnings expectations, and it is currently driven by a single dominant theme, the build-out of artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure. These features make it an ideal subject for learning how research methods are applied to real market questions.

The plan uses a **hybrid, top-down then bottom-up design**. Macroeconomic and sector indicators set the context, fundamental and valuation analysis tests whether prices are supported by earnings, technical analysis reads trend and momentum, and a scenario framework turns the evidence into a forward-looking view. Eight indicators are specified, of which three (forward P/E, EPS growth and revisions, and the 10-year Treasury yield) form the core set. All data comes from free, verifiable public sources such as SEC EDGAR, FRED, S&P Dow Jones Indices and FactSet Earnings Insight.

> **Plan at a glance**
>
> - **Sector:** Information Technology (semiconductors, software and services, hardware).
> - **Methods:** top-down macro, bottom-up fundamental, technical, quantitative (correlation and regression), sentiment, and scenario analysis.
> - **Core indicators:** forward P/E, EPS growth and revisions, 10-year Treasury yield, plus five supporting indicators.
> - **Timeline:** seven working days, 32 hours, eight analytical steps.
> - **Output:** an indicator scorecard and bull, base and bear scenarios that feed Weeks 2 to 6.

