## 3. Sector Selection and Initial Research

### 3.1 Why Information Technology

- **Size and influence:** IT is among the largest sectors in the S&P 500, so its movements materially affect index returns and portfolios.
- **Rich data:** companies report quarterly in detail, with abundant public filings, analyst estimates and industry statistics.
- **Analytical variety:** the sector blends cyclical areas (semiconductors) with recurring-revenue areas (software), allowing several methods to be compared.
- **Relevance:** it is central to current market debates on AI spending, valuation and concentration.

### 3.2 Sector structure

Under the Global Industry Classification Standard (GICS), the IT sector has three industry groups. The companies named are illustrative examples only.

| Industry group | Illustrative companies | Main earnings drivers |
|---|---|---|
| Semiconductors and Semiconductor Equipment | Nvidia, Broadcom, AMD, Micron, Applied Materials, Lam Research | AI accelerator and memory demand, inventory cycles, customer capital spending, export controls, foundry capacity |
| Software and Services | Microsoft, Oracle, Salesforce, ServiceNow, Adobe, Accenture | Subscription and cloud growth, net revenue retention, enterprise IT budgets, AI product monetisation, pricing power |
| Technology Hardware and Equipment | Apple, Cisco, Dell, Arista Networks | Product cycles, data-centre networking and servers, supply chain, tariffs, consumer demand |

*Table 2. Structure of the IT sector under GICS.*

**A classification caveat.** Popular discussion of "tech stocks" spans more than one GICS sector. Alphabet and Meta Platforms sit in Communication Services and Amazon sits in Consumer Discretionary, while payment networks such as Visa and Mastercard were moved from IT to Financials in the 2023 GICS revision. The research will therefore use the GICS IT sector as its formal definition and treat these companies as a separate "related demand" group, particularly when tracking AI-related capital expenditure.

### 3.3 Current market themes

The themes below reflect a review of publicly available brokerage commentary, financial media and company disclosures in October 2026. Specific index levels, multiples and growth rates are deliberately not fixed in this plan because they change daily; they will be retrieved and time-stamped on the day of analysis (Section 8, Step 1).

- **AI as the dominant narrative.** Spending on data centres, accelerators, memory, networking and power has been the main driver of earnings expectations and valuations across semiconductors and hardware, and increasingly software.
- **Debate over the payoff.** Public commentary is divided. Some institutions describe the long-term outlook as strong and driven by durable AI demand, while others warn of bubble-like conditions or argue that the market now needs proof of AI-driven profit growth.
- **Concentration and breadth.** A small number of very large companies account for a large share of sector and index performance, so sector averages can mask wide differences between stocks. Market commentary has also discussed rotation toward other sectors.
- **Interest rates and valuation.** Because much of the sector's value lies in future earnings, expectations for central bank policy and long-term yields remain an important valuation driver.
- **Policy and geopolitics.** Export controls on advanced chips, tariffs and the concentration of advanced chip manufacturing in Taiwan create event risk for the supply chain.
- **Physical constraints.** Electricity supply, cooling and availability of high-bandwidth memory can limit the pace of AI infrastructure expansion.

