## 4. Key Factors Affecting Stock Market Movements

Stock prices reflect expectations about future cash flows and the rate used to discount them. The factors below are grouped by how they reach IT sector prices. Understanding the transmission mechanism is what allows an analyst to choose the right indicator for each factor.

| Category | Factor | How it affects IT stocks |
|---|---|---|
| Macroeconomic | Interest rates and monetary policy | Higher discount rates reduce the present value of distant earnings, which hits long-duration growth stocks hardest. Policy surprises can move the sector sharply. |
| Macroeconomic | Inflation and economic growth | Inflation shapes the rate path. Growth influences corporate IT budgets and consumer device demand. |
| Macroeconomic | US dollar | Many IT firms earn a large share of revenue abroad. A stronger dollar reduces reported foreign revenue. |
| Sector | AI and cloud capital expenditure | Spending by large cloud providers is revenue for chip, server and networking suppliers. Changes in spending guidance move supplier stocks. |
| Sector | Semiconductor cycle | Chip demand and inventory swing between shortage and glut, creating earnings volatility. |
| Sector | Competition and technology shifts | New products or business models can rapidly change market share and pricing power. |
| Policy and geopolitical | Trade controls, tariffs, regulation | Export limits, tariffs and antitrust or data rules can restrict markets or raise costs. |
| Company | Earnings, guidance and margins | Results versus expectations, and management outlook, are the most direct short-term price drivers. |
| Market behaviour | Sentiment, flows and positioning | Crowded positioning and index fund flows can amplify moves in both directions, independent of fundamentals. |

*Table 3. Factors and transmission mechanisms.*

