## 5. Key Market Indicators

The task requires at least three indicators. This plan specifies eight, in two tiers. The **core tier** is the minimum set that must be analysed in full. The **supporting tier** adds cross-checks so that no conclusion rests on a single measure.

| # | Indicator | What it measures | Why it matters for IT | Source and frequency |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 (Core) | Forward P/E of the IT sector versus the S&P 500 and its own 10-year average | Price paid per dollar of expected earnings over the next 12 months | Shows whether the sector is expensive or cheap relative to history and the market | FactSet Earnings Insight (weekly); ETF issuer pages |
| 2 (Core) | EPS growth and estimate revisions (including share of companies beating estimates) | Direction and strength of earnings and of analyst expectations | Earnings and revisions are the main fundamental support for prices | FactSet Earnings Insight (weekly); SEC EDGAR 10-Q and 8-K (quarterly) |
| 3 (Core) | 10-year US Treasury yield and 10-year real yield | Market-implied cost of long-term money | Higher real yields lower the value of long-dated growth cash flows | FRED series DGS10 and DFII10 (daily) |
| 4 (Supporting) | Hyperscaler capital expenditure | Spending by the largest cloud and AI customers | Leading indicator for semiconductor, server and networking demand | Company earnings releases and filings (quarterly) |
| 5 (Supporting) | Semiconductor cycle: PHLX Semiconductor Index (SOX) and global chip sales | Market pricing and actual industry sales of chips | Confirms whether the hardware side of the AI trade is real and broad | Nasdaq (daily); SIA/WSTS sales data (monthly) |
| 6 (Supporting) | Relative strength (sector ETF divided by S&P 500) and breadth (% of stocks above 200-day average) | Trend leadership and how many stocks participate | Separates a broad advance from one driven by a few large names | TradingView or StockCharts (daily) |
| 7 (Supporting) | ISM Manufacturing PMI (new orders) | Direction of business activity and demand | Context for the cyclical parts of the sector | ISM (monthly) |
| 8 (Supporting) | VIX and investor sentiment survey | Expected volatility and investor mood | Highlights fear or complacency that can precede reversals | FRED VIXCLS (daily); AAII survey (weekly) |

*Table 4. Indicator set. Series codes are the identifiers used on the FRED database.*

### 5.1 Interpretation guide

- **Valuation:** a forward P/E in the top decile of its 10-year range implies less margin for error, but is not a timing signal by itself. It must be read alongside earnings growth.
- **Earnings:** rising estimates with a high share of positive surprises supports the trend. Falling estimates while prices rise signals a valuation-driven rally that is more fragile.
- **Rates:** a sustained rise in the real yield is a headwind. A falling real yield is supportive but may reflect weaker growth, so it is checked against the PMI.
- **Confirmation rule:** a conclusion is only considered robust when at least three of the five scorecard categories (Appendix A) agree.

