## 6. Research Methodologies and Rationale

No single method answers all four research questions. The plan therefore combines complementary methods and uses each for the question it is best suited to.

| Method | How it will be applied | Why it was selected | Known limitation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Top-down macro analysis | Start with growth, inflation, rates, dollar and policy; trace their effect to the sector (Table 3). | Answers RQ2. Sector moves are heavily influenced by macro conditions, especially rates. | Macro relationships change over time and are rarely stable. |
| Bottom-up fundamental analysis | Review revenue, margins, EPS growth, cash flow and revisions for the sector and its largest companies; compare valuation with history. | Answers RQ3. Earnings are the ultimate source of long-term returns. | Depends on estimates, which can be wrong or revised sharply. |
| Technical analysis | Moving averages, relative strength, momentum and drawdown to classify the trend state. | Answers RQ1. Captures what investors are actually doing, not just what models imply. | Backward-looking and subjective if patterns are loosely defined. |
| Quantitative analysis | Rolling correlations and a simple regression of sector returns on market returns and yield changes (H1). | Gives objective, testable evidence for or against hypotheses. | Correlation is not causation; results are sensitive to the sample period. |
| Sentiment and positioning | Track volatility, investor surveys and breadth. | Detects crowded trades and extremes in optimism or fear. | Short-term signal with many false alarms. |
| Scenario analysis | Define bull, base and bear cases with triggers linked to the indicators. | Answers RQ4. Handles uncertainty better than a single point forecast. | Scenarios are judgement-based and probabilities are approximate. |

*Table 5. Methods, rationale and limitations.*

### 6.1 How the methods fit together

The analysis flows as a funnel. **Macro analysis** sets the environment. **Fundamental analysis** tests whether prices are justified. **Technical and sentiment analysis** show how the market is behaving right now. **Quantitative tests** check the relationships assumed above. Finally, **scenario analysis** combines the evidence into a view with explicit triggers. Using several methods reduces the risk of confirmation bias, because a conclusion has to survive evidence from different angles.

