## 12. Expected Outputs, Limitations and Ethics

### 12.1 Expected outputs

- A time-stamped baseline of sector data and a trend classification.
- A valuation and earnings assessment with percentile rankings.
- Results of the hypothesis tests (H1 to H3).
- A completed indicator scorecard and three scenarios with triggers.
- A monitoring calendar that carries forward into Weeks 2 to 6, where a company will be valued (Week 2), charts studied in detail (Week 3), risks and diversification assessed (Week 4), and economic indicators examined at greater depth (Week 5).

### 12.2 Limitations

The plan relies on public data and simple tools, so it cannot match the depth of institutional research. Statistical tests use limited samples, scenario judgements are subjective, and markets can move on information that is not in any indicator. Findings should be read as a structured assessment under uncertainty.

### 12.3 Ethics and disclosure

The work uses only public information. No non-public information is used, sources are credited, and the document does not constitute investment advice. Any positions held by the author in relevant securities would be disclosed.

